this video is sponsored by Skillshare  Charlie Kaufman is an American  screenwriter and film director renowned  for his uniquely structured reflexive  and theoretical writing style a style  that pushes against the borders of  conventional storytelling and pursues  new entry points into the creative space  of communication Kaufman has written  films such as adaptation Being John  Malkovich Eternal Sunshine of the  spotless mind  Synecdoche New York animal ISA as well  as several other works  Kaufman's films contain incredible  cinematic technique surrealistic senses  of time confusingly poignant character  developments in many other distinctive  qualities however this will not be so  much about Kaufman's films but rather  Kaufman's apparent philosophy  most of Kaufman's work deals with the  concept of self identity more  specifically our awareness and concern  with who we are and how we are  experienced in the minds of others this  idea can perhaps be neatly expressed  with a quote by 17th century sociologist  Charles Cooley who said I am not who you  think I am  I am not who I think I am I am who I  think you think I am which is to say we  are who we believe others see us as in a  constantly evolving feedback loop  created out of our actions other  people's perceptions of our actions and  our assumptions of their perceptions  throughout his writing Kaufman explores  how we work through this convoluted  house of mirrors attempting to find and  live with our true self  [Music]  just walk like yourself watch what's  this  I gotta go what  we all have sides of ourselves that we  hide we have things that we do in like  that we are ashamed of things we don't  understand or can't seem to control no  one exists without vices without flaws  some flaws and traits so unpleasant and  off from the shared game of normality  that we can sometimes become  insufferable to those who get close  enough to us as a result most of us  exist socially more on the surface of  our self omitting the more complex and  difficult elements of who we are in  order to fit in or experience smoother  social interactions  it is perhaps reasonable to some degree  to play along with this social game and  to withhold some heavier darker  information and qualities of ourselves  so to avoid burdening others with our  dysfunction very well knowing that  everyone is also dealing with their own  however there also needs to be a place  and opportunity to access share and  connect over these dysfunctions for the  same reason be it that everyone is  dealing with them but rarely is anyone  sharing or talking about them we spend  so much of our life trying to contrive  and exist as the best versions of  ourselves in the minds of other people  and thus we can easily become lost or in  a strange state of confusion over who we  really are or who people really are  arguably it is art philosophy and any  other creative act that allows us to  somewhat turn our perspective onto our  true self and dig deeper into the core  of who we really are  as individuals and as a collective art  and philosophy let us say what we cannot  normally they let us meet with others in  the deeper or darker recesses of our  minds talk about what's there and  connect on a level only possible in such  a place however in order to successfully  do any of this we must first be willing  to face and share our self with complete  vulnerability and authenticity or at  least try when writing and discussing  writing Kaufman defends with great  conviction the importance and value of  pursuing and expressing art creativity  and perhaps life in general with honesty  and self truth no matter how convoluted  dumb strange boring or scary we might  feel in the process say who you are  really say it in your life and in your  work  tell someone out there who is lost  someone not yet born someone who won't  be born for 500 years your writing will  be a record of your time it can't help  but be but more importantly if you're  honest about who you are you'll help  that person be less lonely in their  world because that person will recognize  him or herself in you through the  creative process and perhaps in many  other aspects of our daily life it can  be found that in order to do anything  novel or earnest we must risk having our  deepest and most vulnerable qualities be  rejected we must risk sounding dumb  acting weird being wrong or feeling bad  we must risk all of this in order to  ever share anything real about ourselves  and avoid living and dying without the  world ever really seeing who we are in  Kaufman's film adaptation he writes a  film literally about him writing a film  more or less about him Charlie Kaufman  the character in the film struggles  constantly with the feelings that he is  wrong boring cliche self-indulgent and  disingenuous while writing the film he  is in it myself into my screenplay  that's kind of weird self-indulgent it's  narcissistic solipsistic it's pathetic  I'm pathetic I'm fat pathetic I'm sure  you had good reasons Charles you're an  artist to avoid any possibility of  spoiling the film all that needs to be  said here is that Kaufman obviously  still wrote the film in spite of these  things likewise by facing our  insecurities fears confusions and  whatever else by recognizing that we  probably are all of these things and  still muscling through the process of  sharing or expressing ourselves anyway  we might find some truth in our self  worth sharing and more likely than not  we might help others find some truth in  themselves as well  Kaufman often appears to address the  idea that we're all struggling with the  same fundamental thing whether we  realize it or not we are all condemned  by the same consciousness and the same  condition an awareness that we are  trapped in a mind in a body in a world  in a universe thing with no idea what  any of it really is or why it is  your brain right now doesn't know why  your brain right now can wonder why your  brain is right now as these words hit  the part of your brain that processes  words and ideas your brain thinks about  itself wondering what it is somehow  using you as a method of Inquisition  horribly failing and then putting the  burden onto you like how a plant is  confined to its fixed roots in the  ground destined to grow contribute some  purpose towards the ecosystem around it  of which you will never understand or  care to understand and then die off with  a changing of seasons so too do we live  in extra bleak confined to the roots of  our being different than the plant  though we of course live knowing that we  are in the same sort of way stuck in the  ground with no control over the nature  of our condition destined to live and  die with the seasons whether we are  aware of this awareness or not no matter  how rich famous smart or powerful anyone  might be on the surface we all share in  this in Kaufman's film animal ISA the  main character is a successful  motivational speaker almost worshipped  by those he encounters but yet as an  audience we see how broken confused and  flawed he really is for Kaufman rather  than hiding or being dishonest about the  struggle we all go through it is through  the sharing of our experience of this  struggle with authenticity and honesty  that helps us uncover it so we can face  it grapple with it and hopefully  transmuted into something beautiful or  helpful ultimately no matter our efforts  to know who we are and who we aren't is  an incredibly hard constant and abstract  effort it's fair to argue that our true  self is sort of like a target just close  enough to see but too far to ever really  hit always changing or moving away from  us as we live our life and encounter  more and more of the world however  regardless of how hard Kaufman and his  work offers us a reminder that in each  moment we should aim and shoot towards  this target as best we can in our work  our relationships and our everyday life  this vide